  "anomaly_detected": true,
  "confidence": 0.5,
  "anomalies": [
    {
      "category": "other",
      "components": ["R27"],
      "pins": [],
      "nets": ["VMEAS", "ESTOP_SINK_ALL"],
      "observed": "The reference designator R27 is used twice on the board for two different, unrelated components with conflicting values: on the 'Motion Sensing' sheet R27 is a 50k 0.1% resistor in the VMOT->VMEAS voltage divider (alongside R53/R54/R55), while on the 'LED Enable and E-Stop Config' sheet R27 is a 130R series resistor for the LED_RED indicator D8 hanging off the ESTOP_SINK_ALL node.",
      "expected": "Every physical component must carry a unique reference designator so the BOM, netlist and silkscreen agree; a single ref like R27 cannot denote both a 50k precision divider resistor and a 130R LED current-limit resistor.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "Motion Sensing sheet: '50k 0.1% R27' in the R53/R54/R27/R55 divider from VMOT to VMEAS/GND"},
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "LED Enable and E-Stop Config sheet: '130R R27' in series with LED_RED D8 on the ESTOP_SINK_ALL node"}
      ],
      "impact": "Duplicate/conflicting designator corrupts the BOM and silkscreen: an assembler sourcing 'R27' from the BOM may populate the wrong footprint (e.g. a 130R LED resistor dropped into the high-impedance ADC divider, or a 50k resistor in the LED path), breaking input-voltage sensing accuracy or the e-stop indicator, and the two nets share a ref so any correction to one silently mislabels the other.",
      "confidence": 0.5
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The board's architecture matches the specification (16 MHz CAN crystals Y1/Y2, MCP2515 U8/U9, isolated transceivers U10/U11, CDSOT23-SM712 TVS D9/D10, LTC4412 U6, SN74LVC1G07 e-stop buffers, WS2812 chain, ISL54222 mux), and the odd-looking items (GLOBAL_EN, LED polarity, USB diode, switch direction) are documented open issues. The one deviation the drawing supports is a duplicated reference designator R27 carrying two conflicting values (50k divider vs 130R LED resistor)."
}

